$ sudo apt install python3-pip
$ pip install --upgrade pip --break-system-packages

Install virtualenv:
For some reasons the usual pip command didn't work for me. So:
$ sudo apt install python3-virtualenv
Create a virtualenv:
$ virtualenv rapids-minimal /choose any name you like instead of rapids-minimal
Enter the virtualenv:
$ source rapids-minimal/bin/activate
Install the pacakges:
$ pip install matplotlib pandas numpy scikit-learn seaborn jupyterlab
Install the latest stable rapids packages you need from here:
https://docs.rapids.ai/install/#system-req
I kept it minimal as it is Lubuntu already:
$ pip install     "cudf-cu13==26.6.*" "cuml-cu13==26.6.*"
Pytorch for checking:
https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/
Match the CUDA version:
$ pip3 install torch torchvision --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu132

Then start the jupyter lab ot jupyter notebook. Depends. I used jupyter lab.

Lubuntu is a great lightweigth distro and it is Ubuntu underneath so the full support is not in question. Some manual labour and more clicking needed compared to 
Ubuntu but that is just fine if someone knows what they're doing. If you want project specific distro Lubuntu saves more resources at the end of the day and obviously
it is disposable once your project is finished. But with the full NVIDIA driver support it is a fast and reliable solution. A high end PC should handle Ubuntu just 
fine. This is just self-interest and testing. The file is provided and I uploaded the jupyer file. Basically the same as the Ubuntu 26.04 jupyter file. After all they
are Ubuntu distros. But on a laptop with the right GPU Lubuntu can be preferred as it is lighter.

I hope this helps everone who is intersted in this project! Enjoy! Big thanks to the Ubuntu Team to make our lives easier with the right driver solutions! 
